  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nThere are a few features of note dominating the steering flow across \nCONUS.A deepening PV anomaly in the PNW merging with the \nremnants of Rachel in the desert SW over the next day. This will\ndevelop into a cutoff/upper low over the four corners region by\nTuesday night with a strong 120kt+ jet streak arcing NE into \nthe Great Lakes atop an upper level ridge that will push \nnortheast out of Mexico into the Lower Mississippi basin in the\nwake of the remnants of Isaias. With this, ejecting shortwaves \nwill quickly move through the developing SW flow aloft into the \nDakotas overnight tonight and into Tuesday bring ample forcing \nfor rain (mainly along the intl border). Through the later half \nof the week the cutoff low begins to eject east with the AI ENS\nguidance showing decent agreement with timing of the wave but \nslight differences in its track and potential interaction with \na wave moving south through the Canadian prairies by Thursday. \nOverall the interaction looks to favor any more robust QPF to \nour east into the Great Lakes but there remain scenarios where \nwe still see measurable QPF, those are not the favored outcome \nanymore however. Behind this wave will be a notable cool down \nwith 850mb temps well below 0C with differences then arising \ninto next weekend regarding the evolution of the synoptic \npattern.\n\n- Rain tonight through Monday night\n\nWhile PWAT's will only marginally increase over the next 24 hours \nfrom the current 1.1\" to around 1.3\" forcing via isentropic \nascent and 850mb WAA along with transient vorticity advection \nfrom the remnants of Rachel will race northeast. The main window\nfor heavier rates will be from 12z Monday to 06z Tuesday along \nand north of HWY 2. Nearly all of the area has a greater than \n50% chance for over a tenth on an inch except in the far south \nwith areas north of HWY 2 seeing upwards of a 40% chance for \nover half an inch. Given very weak upright instability this \nshould be more of a cool season type stratiform rain with only \noccasional embedded lightning.\n\n- Fire Weather\n\nLater in the week as the cutoff/upper low moves northeast into the \nmidwest and the southeast moving Canadian wave interact, a cold\nfront will swing through the Dakotas with some chances for rain\nThursday into Friday, though more than a tenth to quarter inch \nlooks unlikely at this point. Behind this cold front comes a \ndrier airmass with MinRH Friday into the weekend looking to fall\ninto the 30s if not 20s percent range. Between the Canadian \nwave and a building thermal ridge across MT/WY will be enhanced\nnortherly low level flow with resulting surface winds of \n20-30mph. The question is do the strong winds and low RH ever \noverlap and are fuels even favorable for quick fire spread? \nThese question will be answered in the coming days but enhanced \nfire weather for at least one or two days Thursday to Sunday \nseems probable. \n\n- Cool Down\n\nWhile any fire weather will likely steal the attention should it \noccur this first real shot of Canadian air for the season should\nbring morning lows in the lower 30s and upper 20s late this \nweek into next weekend. Should this happen it would end the \ngrowing season and any frost/freeze headlines. Taking an initial\npeak it may be too windy for frost formation most mornings so \nit could become a pattern of freeze warning or nothing should it\nget cold enough to warrant headlines.",
